Per-stage share of total NFMCD.fit() time, OLD vs NEW fusion (n=20000):
  old: fusion 94.6%  rest(struct+alpha+fcm) 5.4%  total 20.92s
  new: fusion 93.0%  rest(struct+alpha+fcm) 7.0%  total 16.00s

CORRECTION to scale_summary.log's framing (n=20000, n_paired=20000, dim_t=384, dim_v=512):
The earlier scaling experiment measured `fusion.fuse()` as one 94%-of-total block and
attributed it to the per-node loop. Isolating the two pieces inside fuse() directly shows
that's only partly right:
  _fit_alignment (PCA+CCA fit, SHARED code, unaffected by vectorization): 14.27s
  per-node assembly loop (the thing vectorized here):                      5.45s -> 0.27s (20.5x)
_fit_alignment dominates (14.27 / 19.7 = 72% of old fuse() time) because its PCA rank cap
(n_paired // pca_rank_div, default pca_rank_div=4) hits the embedding-dimension ceiling
(384/512) well before n=20000, making it a near-full-rank PCA + CCA fit on a 20000-row
matrix -- genuine O(n) linear algebra that no amount of loop-vectorization touches.
The loop vectorization is real and correctly targeted (20.5x speedup, verified in
isolation above) but was only ~28% of fuse()'s time at this size, not ~94% -- hence the
modest 1.3x end-to-end speedup rather than something larger. The TRUE bottleneck at large
n is `_fit_alignment`'s PCA/CCA fit, not the loop. Recommendation: if further speedup is
wanted, the next target is capping PCA rank more aggressively at large n_paired (e.g. a
fixed cap independent of n_paired, or a randomized-SVD PCA) rather than further work on
the assembly step, which is no longer the dominant cost.
